# xhci98.sys amd64 import allowlist - module/symbol pairs the NT 5.2 amd64
# build may import.
#
# The amd64 sibling of xhci98-imports.allow. Same grammar, same enforcement,
# different rows and a different evidence rule; check-imports.ps1 selects
# between the two on the architecture of the image it is gating.
#
# **WHY A SEPARATE FILE RATHER THAN A COLUMN** (the owner's decision,
# 2026-09-09, roadmap task 21.3). Two reasons, and the second is the stronger.
# Of the thirteen distinct kernel/HAL rows across both architectures only two
# are shared, so a merged file would be two lists sharing a header. And the
# other file's [deny] section is justified end to end by "not exported on
# Win98" and "Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000": reasoning
# about two 32-bit operating systems that does not transfer here, where most of
# those APIs genuinely exist. Merging would have meant re-justifying 27 denials
# against a target none of them was written about.
#
# Format is xhci98-imports.allow's, unchanged:
#
#   MODULE!SYMBOL   FLAVORS   REQUIREMENT   evidence notes to end of line
#
# Pairs, not names, for the same reason as there: the PE import descriptor
# names the providing module, so ntoskrnl.exe!X and HAL.dll!X are different
# imports and only one of them resolves. Module names match
# case-insensitively, symbol names exactly.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE EVIDENCE RULE HERE IS STRONGER THAN THE WINDOWS 98 ONE, NOT WEAKER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The other file's standing rule is that every pair must carry Windows 98
# evidence of its own - a precedent binary importing the same pair, or the
# symbol appearing in ntkern.vxd's name table. That rule is a *compensation for
# an unreadable target*: Windows 98 builds its NT-style export tables at run
# time in ntkern.vxd rather than reading them from a file, so there is no
# export table on disk to resolve against and both sources are proxies whose
# absence proves nothing.
#
# NT 5.2 amd64 has a real export table. So the rule here is direct resolution:
# **every ntoskrnl.exe or hal.dll pair must appear in the export table of every
# baseline image in winxp64-baselines.expected** - both kernels and the one
# HAL - as a real code export rather than a forwarder. That is dispositive in a
# way no Windows 98 evidence ever is, which is why no proxy rule is stated
# here and none is wanted. It is the same check task 22.2 ran for the 6.0 and
# 6.1 x86 kernels.
#
# The consequence to keep in mind: when the baselines are not staged in a
# working copy, an amd64 row has NO evidence behind it at all. The gate warns
# rather than failing in that case, exactly as the x86 half does, and a build
# taken that way has not been through this rule.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE FLAVORS COLUMN CANNOT DO HERE, AND IT MATTERS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# On x86, HAL.dll!WRITE_PORT_UCHAR is a `qemu required` row and is described
# there as "what keeps the import out of every binary a user can install" - the
# whole of task 13-L.1's enforcement, and the reason the ThinkPad E460's Code 2
# build can never ship.
#
# **On amd64 that row has no counterpart, because the import does not exist.**
# The compiler emits the `out` instruction inline instead of calling the HAL
# export, so the qemu flavour's import table is byte-for-byte the same set as
# the release flavour's. Measured 2026-09-09 on all three amd64 flavours: seven
# pairs each, identical.
#
# So the import gate cannot tell an amd64 qemu build from an amd64 release
# build, and nothing in this file can be written to make it. The guarantee
# still holds - scripts\check-flavour-marker.ps1 reads XHCI98_FLAVOUR_* out of
# every linked image and make-package.ps1 refuses the qemu flavour by name -
# but on amd64 it rests on the in-image marker alone where on x86 it rests on
# the marker and an import. That asymmetry is recorded deliberately (the
# owner's decision, 2026-09-09) so that nobody later reads this gate as
# covering something it cannot. Design record 11 section 8 carries it too.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ROWS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Five kernel/HAL pairs, against x86's nine plus the qemu row. The USBPORT.SYS
# pairs are NOT listed here for the same reason they are not listed there: they
# are read from scripts\usbport-lib\usbport-imports.expected, which the .def
# and both import libraries are generated from, so a second hand-maintained
# copy cannot drift out of step with them. Design record 11's M1 read the same
# two names at the same two ordinals off the amd64 usbport.sys, undecorated.
#
# Every "nt52-amd64-export" tag below was re-derived by the gate itself on
# 2026-09-09 against ntoskrnl.exe, ntkrnlmp.exe and hal.dll 5.2.3790.3959, and
# each hint is the one the linker recorded in our own binary - they match the
# real export hints, which is a cross-check the x86 rows cannot make.

[imports]

# --- The register and stall primitives -------------------------------------
# Only one of the x86 pair survives into amd64. READ_REGISTER_ULONG and
# WRITE_REGISTER_ULONG are compiler intrinsics here and are emitted inline, so
# they are not imported and must not be listed: a `required` row for a symbol
# the binary cannot import would fail every build.
HAL.dll!KeStallExecutionProcessor         all  required  nt52-amd64-export (hint 3B, hal.dll RVA 0x10320); the only HAL import the amd64 build has

# --- The controller lock (src\xhci_cmd.c) ----------------------------------
# **These two replace the x86 HAL pair and the change is structural, not a
# rename.** On x86 the DDK's KeAcquireSpinLock(a,b) macro expands to
# HAL.dll!KfAcquireSpinLock, a __fastcall HAL export that exists only on i386.
# On amd64 the same source line resolves to ntoskrnl.exe!KeAcquireSpinLockRaise
# ToDpc and ntoskrnl.exe!KeReleaseSpinLock - a different module as well as a
# different name, which is exactly the case the "pairs, not names" rule is for.
# KeInitializeSpinLock and HAL.dll!KeGetCurrentIrql are inlined here and so are
# absent, as is ntoskrnl.exe!InterlockedIncrement, which is an intrinsic.
ntoskrnl.exe!KeAcquireSpinLockRaiseToDpc  all  required  nt52-amd64-export (hint 20C, ntoskrnl RVA 0x38000 / ntkrnlmp 0x25010)
ntoskrnl.exe!KeReleaseSpinLock            all  required  nt52-amd64-export (hint 265, ntoskrnl RVA 0x380A0 / ntkrnlmp 0x24FF0)

# --- The log channel (src\xhci_log.c, src\xhci_dispatch.c) -----------------
# Required in every flavour by task 11-V.9's DebugView sink, as on x86.
ntoskrnl.exe!DbgPrint                     all  required  nt52-amd64-export (hint 31, ntoskrnl RVA 0x595F0 / ntkrnlmp 0x56660)

# --- The /GS stack cookie handler ------------------------------------------
# **Nothing in this driver calls KeBugCheckEx, and this row is not a licence to
# start.** It is pulled in by the compiler, not by the source: WDK 7.1 enables
# /GS buffer security checks for amd64, so every function with a stack buffer
# gets a cookie check, and __security_check_cookie jumps to __report_gsfailure
# on a mismatch, which is the single call site - one, in the whole of .text, at
# RVA 0x25218 in the release image, reached by nothing else. It issues
# KeBugCheckEx(0xF7, ...) = DRIVER_OVERRAN_STACK_BUFFER.
#
# It is therefore unreachable except on a genuine stack-buffer overrun, and it
# is a safety feature the x86 binary CANNOT have: MSVC 6.0 predates /GS
# entirely, so a stack smash there corrupts silently where here it becomes an
# attributable bugcheck. That is the whole reason this row is accepted rather
# than the flag being turned off to match x86.
#
# If a second call site ever appears, that is a real call from this driver's
# own code and needs its own decision - the same discipline the other file
# applies to DbgPrint's single emit site.
ntoskrnl.exe!KeBugCheckEx                 all  required  nt52-amd64-export (hint 213, ntoskrnl RVA 0x41690 / ntkrnlmp 0x2E950); /GS __report_gsfailure only, one call site, bugcheck 0xF7

[deny]
#
# **THE x86 FILE'S DENIALS ARE NOT REPEATED HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE
# SPLIT.** All 27 of them are justified by absence on Windows 98 (Oney page
# cites) or by "Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000". Neither
# argument holds on NT 5.2 amd64: Windows 98 is not a target of this binary at
# all, and the XP-and-later APIs those rows refuse are present here. Copying
# them across would have produced a file full of refusals with false reasons,
# which is worse than a short list with true ones.
#
# What survives the change of target is the one denial that is about this
# project's own architecture rather than about an operating system's vintage.
#
ExAllocatePool              Option A needs no private pool at all - fixed metadata belongs in the usbport-allocated miniport and common-buffer extensions (docs\contributing\design\04-controller-common-buffer.md). AGENTS.md states the rule as policy rather than as a missing export, and it is policy on every target: an allocation here would be this driver doing usbport's job. The same reasoning covers the tagged forms below.
ExAllocatePoolWithTag       Same rule as ExAllocatePool. On amd64 the DDK's POOL_TAGGING rewrite that makes this the *default* spelling on x86 is not the reason for the denial - the denial is the no-private-pool policy itself.
ExFreePool                  Nothing is allocated, so nothing is freed.
ExFreePoolWithTag           Same.
KeAcquireInterruptSpinLock  Under Option A usbport owns the interrupt object, so the miniport has no business synchronising against it directly. Unlike the x86 file's row, this is NOT denied here because it blocks a load - it exists on NT 5.2 - it is denied because reaching for it means the miniport has taken on something usbport owns.
KeReleaseInterruptSpinLock  Same.
MmMapLockedPagesSpecifyCache  Option B only. Under Option A every buffer this driver touches is one usbport mapped and handed over.
